A||||9||Patrick||Inner clearance for rear wheels and tires
Z||000000||Patrick||08-17-99||11:50 PM||||<br>My ‘87 Fiero has 14 x 6 inch wheels originally from a Buick. I've noticed that the lead weights used for wheel balancing on the inner rim have been almost completely sheared off by the rear strut flange that the mounting bolts go through (where it mounts onto the knuckle). This kind of surprised me when I first discovered it. To make a little more clearance I had to file down a bit of the strut flange that was protruding out further from the knuckle than was necessary. There's only about a quarter of an inch of clearance between the inner wheel rim and the strut/knuckle mount. <p>I don't know how much difference, if any, there might be on the offset of various GM wheels, but is this the normal clearance on the "stock" 14 inch Fiero wheels?<p>All this talk in the other threads about tires has also inspired me to replace the bald tires on this car. Right now it has 205x60x14 tires all around. They look fine on the front, but I've always hated the way they look on the back. The rear wheel wells are so big that these tires look silly on the back. There's way too much empty space above and to the front and back of the rear tires for my liking. <p>I'm interested to hear if anyone has installed <b>235</b>x60x14, or maybe 225x<b>70</b>x14 tires on the back of their Fieros and whether or not there were any clearance problems with the struts or the bottom of the springs, etc. <p>Please give me some feedback before I slide into someone here on the always wet Vancouver pavement.<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p><p><br>
Z||000001||theogre||08-18-99||07:19 AM||||the offset on those wheels is wrong. 1/4 inch is not enough. There is a small amount of play in the hubs and they sould still contact the rim/wheights on a hard turn.<p>you'll probly need to change rims to go up to a 235.  
Z||000002||batboy||08-18-99||07:40 AM||||Hey Patrick, I had 245x60x14 tires on factory wheels on the rear of my Batmobile when I bought it. One side was rubbing very slightly on the inside of the tire on sharp curves, but other people claim they don't have any trouble with this size tire, maybe because that heavy V8 hunkers the rear down more on my car. Anyway, I have 235x60x14 Goodyear Eagles on the rear now on the same stock rims and they work and look excellent. Plenty of clearance. Ogre may be right about the offset being incorrect on those Buick wheels.
Z||000003||Patrick||08-18-99||10:53 PM||||<br>Hmmm.... batboy, any chance that you would happen to know how much clearance you have between the inside rim and the strut/knuckle mount?<p>Is there such a thing as a "spacer" that could be used between the wheel hub and the wheel? I'm only talking about a quarter inch maximum here. This may not be the recommended solution if my clearance is not quite enough, but I'm reluctant to replace the wheels simply because there is a fraction of an inch less clearance than optimal.<p>
Z||000004||batboy||08-19-99||06:07 AM||||Pat, yes they do make a spacer, but they usually are made of aluminum and I wouldn't recommend using them. I've heard that they will work the lug nuts loose and cause problems like that. I was just surfing the Forum this morning before heading to work, but I did run out just now with a flashlight. I'll need to jack the car up to get under there for a closer look, but there appears to be much more clearance than a 1/4 inch. You might be able to have those tires rebalanced and have the weights positioned differently. They have glue on weights specially made for custom alumunim wheels. They probably need rebalanced anyway since some metal was sheared away.
Z||000005||DJRice||08-19-99||07:31 AM||||I will be the first to admit that I dont know much about tires.  I just got a new set of tires on my Fiero.  They are 215x60x14s all the way around.  I hope this is isnt way out of line with the norm, But that was the size that was on there when I bought the car and that was what Sears put right back on.<p>BTW, If anyone wants to post a quick explanation of the tire size numbers, I'd appreciate it.  I'm always looking to get better educated.  I know what the 14 is, Its the 215x60 that I have no clue about.<p>Thanks<br>
Z||000006||theogre||08-19-99||09:00 AM||||xxx/yyRdd nnS<p>xxx the section width of the tire in mm when mounted on a specific width rim. This is the width at the widest point of the side wall, Not the tread.<p>yy the hight of the sidewall as a percent of section width. <p>R radial tire<p>dd rim diameter in inches<p>nn a number that is useless to most people.<p>S "speed rating" of tire. The tire was tested on a machine at a test speed for 30 minutes and didn't blow up. Do Not use this number alone to determine maximum running speed for road/track use.<p>nnS may apear before or after the main number set.<p>Manufacturer data will tell you what rim the tire was measured on. for example a 195/75R14 is usually measured on a 6 inch wide rim, but may fit safely on rims from 5-7 inches wide. Of course changing the rim width will change the section width. Specific data is available on the manufacturors website for many brands.<p>Never rely on the section width as an indication of tread width. They are only marginally related. a 195 and a 205 tire can actually have the same tread width. Tires in diferent styles/brands can have a tread width range of as much as an inch and still all have the same section width.
Z||000007||Patrick||08-19-99||01:44 PM||||<br>Thanks batboy. Please don't go out of your way to check your clearance for me. There's no huge rush. Rummaging around in the dark with a flashlight is going beyond the call of duty.<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>I'm familiar with those stick-on weights that you mentioned and I agree that they'd probably work fine for this application. <p>I think that if my present wheels were actually going to contact the strut/knuckle mount, they'd have done it by now. Besides, there are still remnants of the lead weights left on the inner rim, so there is SOME clearance.<p>I'm more concerned with the possibility of larger diameter, wider tires rubbing the bottom of the springs.<p>Again, there's no big rush for this info. I've been driving (like a fool) on these bald tires for months and months now. A couple more weeks won't make any difference.<p><p>
Z||000008||batboy||08-19-99||09:41 PM||||Pat, I checked out the inner clearance on my rear wheels and it appears to be approximately 3/4 inch, or about 20 mm for our non-USA friends, on my factory alloy 14 inch Fiero wheels. Now buy some new tire...jeez! LOL! Seriously, we don't want you ending up in an accident and bending up that Fiero.
Z||000009||Patrick||08-20-99||12:48 AM||||<br>Thanks batboy, I definitely will get new tires before the autumn monsoons start. Not to be confused with the winter, spring, or summer monsoons that we usually get here.<IMG SRC="/ubb/biggrin.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p> 
